    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d253">

                <pb facs="0404" n="312" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v01/pg312"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72114A/185d</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Stovall</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-09-23T13:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 23, 1918, 1 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2774. For Garrett:<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d253-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>It seems almost unnecessary to caution you against entertaining any overtures for
                    or reference to peace which may be introduced <pb facs="0405" n="313"
                        xml:id="pg313"/> by the German or Austrian delegates during the
                    prisoner-of-war conferences.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d253-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d253-f1" n="1" xml:id="d253-f1-content">
                <p>
                    John W. Garrett, American Minister in the
                    Netherlands, Chairman of the American Commission sent to Berne to negotiate with
                    a German commission an agreement relating to prisoners of war; see <ref
                        target="frus1918Supp02">Supplement 2</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d253-f2" n="1" xml:id="d253-f2-content">
                <p>In a telegram dated Oct. 5, Mr. Garrett
                    reported: “Mensing, one of the German delegates and son-in-law of Vice Consul
                        McNally in charge at Zurich, has tried through his
                    father-in-law to find out whether I would see him privately. I have given him to
                    understand that I would not. Somewhat similar approaches have been made to Mr.
                        Davis [military attaché?] with a like
                    result.” This action was approved by telegram No. 3106 from the Acting Secretary
                    of State, Oct. 11. (File No. 763.72114A/222.)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
